 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Texture synthesis and morphing is a time-consuming process. Partial texture synthesis using binary masks and dilation radius $r_d$ provides efficiency gains. A dilation radius of $r_d = 10$ yields high-quality textures while resulting in significant time savings [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Texture synthesis and morphing is a time-consuming process [S1, unofficial]. Partial texture synthesis using binary masks and dilation radius $r_d$ provides efficiency gains [S1, unofficial]. A dilation radius of $r_d = 10$ yields high-quality textures while resulting in significant time savings [S1, unofficial].
 *   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storage for the scalar data set, binary object mask, weight maps, transformation functions, and additional 2D opacity textures [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Enables encoding of additional information (e.g., fiber direction, cellular abnormalities) throughout the volume in 3D. Provides smooth transitions between texture features using a sophisticated morphing operator. Supports arbitrary mixtures of color and opacity [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Perception of partially transparent solid textures is difficult for the human visual system. The geometry of semi-transparent layers is harder to perceive than with conventional transfer functions. Perception of nested textured regions is difficult without screen-door transparency [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When recognition of material types and properties is more important than exact 3D structure. When additional attributes need to be encoded in the volume beyond color and opacity [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When precise 3D structure of semi-transparent layers is critical. When computational resources are limited and texture synthesis is too slow [S1, unofficial].
